@startuml
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor black
skinparam nodesep 45
skinparam ranksep 55
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor white
  BorderColor #90A4AE
  FontColor #455A64
}
skinparam card {
  BackgroundColor #E8F4FD
  BorderColor #2196F3
  FontColor #1565C0
}

title ttt — the same message at three levels\nleft: a player asks to play cell 5 · right: the server pushes the new board

rectangle "**1 — APPLICATION**   a value in memory, checked by the compiler" as L1 {
  card app_req [
  **client -> server**
  ====
  ""Request::Move(5)""
  ----
  An enum value: which variant it is,
  plus its payload (here a ""u8"").
  You cannot build a nonsense one —
  ""Request::Move(hello)"" does not compile.
  ]
  card app_upd [
  **server -> client**
  ====
  ""Update::Board(cells)""
  ----
  An enum value carrying a ""String""
  of exactly 9 characters:
  ""X2O456789""
  ]
  app_req -[hidden]right- app_upd
}

rectangle "**2 — WIRE FORMAT**   one line of text per message (""core/src/wire.rs"")" as L2 {
  card wire_req [
  ""MOVE 5<U+005C>n""
  ----
  A verb, a space, the argument —
  then the newline that ENDS the message.
  ]
  card wire_upd [
  ""STATE X2O456789<U+005C>n""
  ----
  Same shape: verb, space, argument, newline.
  ]
  wire_req -[hidden]right- wire_upd
}

rectangle "**3 — TCP**   one long stream of bytes, and nothing else" as L3 {
  card tcp_req [
  ""4D 4F 56 45 20 35 0A""
  "" M  O  V  E sp  5 <U+005C>n""
  ----
  **7 bytes.** TCP has no idea that this
  is a message. It carries bytes, in order,
  and that is its whole job.
  ]
  card tcp_upd [
  ""53 54 41 54 45 20 58 32 4F 34 35 36 37 38 39 0A""
  "" S  T  A  T  E sp  X  2  O  4  5  6  7  8  9 <U+005C>n""
  ----
  **16 bytes.** Six for the verb and its space,
  nine for the board, one for the newline.
  ]
  tcp_req -[hidden]right- tcp_upd
}

app_req -down-> wire_req : ""encode_request(&request)""\nbuilds the String
wire_req -down-> tcp_req : ""write_all(frame.as_bytes())""\nthe String becomes bytes

tcp_upd -up-> wire_upd : ""BufReader::lines()""\ncuts the stream at each newline
wire_upd -up-> app_upd : ""decode_update(&line)""\ntext becomes a value again

legend bottom
  Going **down** is encoding, going **up** is decoding — and each direction does both, one at each end.
  Only level 2 ever deals in text: above it everything is a typed value, below it everything is bytes.
  ""sp"" is the space character (0x20) and ""<U+005C>n"" the newline (0x0A) — the two bytes you cannot see on screen.
endlegend

@enduml
